  "h1": "Tenants in Common vs Joint Tenancy Property Rules",
  "introText": "Joint tenancy automatically passes an owner's share of property to the surviving co-owners upon death, whereas tenants in common hold separate shares that pass to chosen heirs through an estate.\n\nState law establishes the legal default for how co-owners hold title, meaning local statutes dictate the outcome when a deed lacks clear survivorship wording. Federal tax rules under the Internal Revenue Code (IRC) also treat property differently at death depending on whether the co-owners are married spouses, how much each person paid toward the purchase price, and whether the asset sits in a community property state.\n\nNeither title arrangement produces an automatic federal tax victory. The best legal form depends entirely on family relationships, purchase financing, and estate planning goals.",
  "tableHeading": "What Happens to Each Owner's Share at Death",
